# libsyntax89

Typed ordered syntax DAGs for strict ISO C89. `libsyntax89` stores nodes
(stable id, client kind, optional source span) and ordered structural edges
(parent, client role, child) and nothing else. It never interprets kinds or
roles, owns source text, resolves names, types, evaluates, or prescribes a
grammar.

```text
syntax
 ├─ nodes
 │   ├─ stable id
 │   ├─ client-defined kind
 │   └─ optional source span
 └─ edges
     ├─ client-defined role
     ├─ source node
     ├─ destination node
     └─ insertion order
```

A call expression is just a shape:

```text
CALL
 ├─ CALLEE ──> NAME
 ├─ ARG    ──> INT
 └─ ARG    ──> NAME
```

The library understands ordering and structure, not what `CALL` means.

## Lifecycle

```text
UNINITIALIZED --init--> BUILDING --freeze--> FROZEN --destroy--> end
```

- BUILDING admits `syntax89_add_node`, `syntax89_add_child`, and
  `syntax89_set_root`.
- FROZEN rejects structural mutation with `SYNTAX89_ESTATE` and admits
  queries, iteration, traversal, and validation.
- There is no deletion and no in-place surgery. A transformation builds a new
  graph from a frozen source graph.

## Types

| Type | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `syntax89_id`, `syntax89_kind`, `syntax89_role` | abstract `unsigned long` names |
| `syntax89_span` | half-open byte range `[begin, end)` in external source |
| `syntax89_node_info` | kind and span of one node |
| `syntax89_allocator` | injectable `alloc`/`realloc`/`free`; NULL selects libc |
| `syntax89_graph` | caller-owned graph handle; fields are private state |
| `syntax89_child_iter` | allocation-free child iterator |
| `syntax89_validation` | error status plus offending node pair |

Node id `0` is the only reserved value and never names a node. Kind and role
values are opaque; `0` is an ordinary kind and an ordinary role.

## Semantics

- `syntax89_add_node` appends a node, returns a fresh nonzero id, preserves
  kind and span exactly, and never changes earlier ids.
- `syntax89_add_child` appends after the parent's existing edges. Absolute
  child order and role-filtered relative order both follow insertion order.
- Shared children are ordinary: one node, several incoming edges. Node count
  counts it once; edge count counts every edge.
- `syntax89_validate` proves that a root exists and names a node, the root has
  no incoming edge, the structural edges are acyclic, and every node is
  reachable from the root. Errors, in precedence order: `EINVAL`, `ESTATE`,
  `EGRAPH` (root), `ECYCLE`, `EGRAPH` (unreachable), `ENOMEM`.
- `syntax89_freeze` validates and then marks the graph FROZEN. Freezing twice
  is an idempotent `SYNTAX89_OK`. Any failure leaves the graph BUILDING and
  logically unchanged.
- `syntax89_walk_nodes_pre/post` visit each node once; `syntax89_walk_edges_
  pre/post` visit each structural occurrence. All four are iterative, detect
  cycles, and follow insertion order.
- Every operation completes fully or changes nothing observable. IDs are
  nonzero, stable for the graph lifetime, and never reused.

## Build and test

- `just build` — build `build/libsyntax89.a`.
- `just smoke` — one end-to-end path.
- `just unit` — unit programs, one per concern.
- `just model` — differential tests against an independent reference model.
- `just generated` — generated mutation sequences.
- `just fault` — allocation failure at every allocation point.
- `just stress` — deep, wide, and highly shared graphs.
- `just deep-test` — the 100,000-deep chain.
- `just cpp-check` — C++23 header compile checks.
- `just test` — smoke, unit, model, generated, fault.
- `just matrix` — gcc/clang x C89/C23 plus optimization levels.
- `just sanitize` — ASan + UBSan run of the fast suite.
- `just valgrind` — memcheck run of the fast suite.
- `just coverage` — 100% line and branch coverage gate on `src/`.
- `just audit` — symbol audit: exact export set, documented libc references.
- `just api-coverage` — every public function is named by a test.
- `just shell-selftest` — scripts abort on failure under `sh` invocation.
- `just coverage-selftest` — the coverage gate rejects incomplete reports.
- `just build-selftest` — no stale objects survive a deleted source.
- `just check` — green gate, lint, tests, probes, audits, coverage, and the
  gate self-checks.
- `just release` — check plus matrix, sanitizers, stress, deep, valgrind.

## Non-goals

Lexing, parsing, grammar definitions, parser generation, token storage, source
text ownership, symbol tables, scopes, name resolution, type systems,
evaluation, interpretation, bytecode, generic IR, arbitrary graph databases,
generic annotations, schemas, serialization, pretty printing, syntax
formatting, diffing, incremental parsing, deletion, and in-place rewrite. A
lossless CST layer, references/annotations, and declarative schemas are
separate future libraries.
